{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the J3 follow-up at head 7e2d31f3a9da3bae190829fb17e2af12b6e38a58, tree cd9928f4da9b95e2aa8795dfee4e75ec84f9ae29, for request 25b40ad2 as amended by ruling 57876548, and review request 9770ee27. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds seven commits to Main 9e313eff. I read the implementation note first. Then I read the source and test diff, the Go helper, the README, the architecture page, the earlier J3 note and the sealed evidence.\n\n## Against request 25b40ad2\n\n1. Recovery. Resolved.\n   - `containerRunner.find` ages the owned run directory by its birth time, or by its modification time when the filesystem has no birth time. `ServiceOptions.recoveryGraceSeconds` defaults to 120 and accepts whole seconds from 0 to 86,400. The note and `docs/architecture.md` state the default.\n   - Inside the grace, a missing or corrupt record returns `running`. After the grace, `InvalidRunRecord` becomes `RunRecordCorruption`. The service then keeps `check-error` with reason `run-lost` and logs `record-corrupt` with the private path.\n   - A claim that names another job ends the same way. The service logs `record-corrupt` with the path of `ask.json`.\n   - Engine errors, unreadable directory metadata and transient reads of other record files still wait. No recovery path deletes a file, resets a job or calls `run` again.\n   - `container-recovery.test.ts` runs six cases on a real private filesystem:\n     - an empty directory that a paused creator fills after the loss;\n     - five malformed, empty, oversized and wrong-run claims;\n     - an unopenable claim, which is a Unix socket;\n     - a corrupt terminal report;\n     - an authenticated record for a different job;\n     - an unavailable engine, which still waits after the grace.\n   - Each case asserts zero launches. Each terminal case also asserts that the record bytes stay unchanged.\n2. Claim size, under ruling 57876548. Resolved.\n   - `claim-too-large` joins `ERRORS` in `outcome.ts`.\n   - `containerRunner.run` throws `ClaimTooLargeRefusal` before it creates the run directory. The service keeps `check-error` with reason `claim-too-large` and logs the event `claim-too-large` on the `run` step.\n   - The witness in `container-records.test.ts` asserts the signed reason field, one log event, zero `find` calls and no runner root on disk. The kept outcome carries no second cause field.\n3. Command output. Resolved.\n   - The supported ctr profile now runs each configured step through `artroom-git-relay step`. The helper refuses any UID except 65532. It duplicates stdout onto stderr and then calls `exec` on the configured argv, so the job's exit status stays its own.\n   - If `exec` fails, the helper restores the saved stderr and writes one fixed line with status 125. The host then refuses the run, because the status is nonzero and stderr is not empty.\n   - `requireContainerExit` keeps the earlier refusals: a negative status, status 255, and a nonzero status with stderr. Trusted Git reads keep the `exec` path with its fixed environment.\n   - `relay_test.go` covers combined streams with status 17, literal argv, a missing executable, a missing interpreter, status 255 and SIGKILL. `container-streams.test.ts` covers the host side.\n   - The README names the supported ctr profile as the one that combines streams. It also says that profiles that keep streams separate still lose a nonzero status that has stderr.\n4. Manifest. Resolved. See the next section.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean at 7e2d31f3. A merge onto main 9e313eff gives the head tree cd9928f4.\n- From the gate commit d2a81c76 to the head, only the new note and the manifest change. From the physical witness commit 02eeceb5 to d2a81c76, only the two notes change.\n- The gate receipt at d2a81c76, tree 8862c2ee: exit 0, complete, 1,204.9 seconds, and the same head after the run.\n- The manifest has SHA-256 d2ddacda and 26,140 bytes. It follows ruling 4e199bd8:\n  - all 184 listed files match by size and SHA-256;\n  - the six cited directories match their digests, and each one's SHA256SUMS reruns clean, with no unlisted file;\n  - the root SHA256SUMS covers the other 886 files, with none unlisted and none writable. With the root file, the collection has 887 files, as the request states.\n- `physical-j3-1` at 02eeceb5 used the local TEST image c665e310:\n  - eight direct jobs ran, eight origins closed, and the Gateway saw zero updates;\n  - the `real-failure` step writes `bad` to stderr, and its terminal record has status 1 and line `bad`, judged `failed`;\n  - status 17 stays 17, and the missing executable ends `lost` with no step;\n  - the Native failing run has status 1 and line `bad`. The selected Native case passed, and its source asserts `required-check-failed` and an unchanged `refs/heads/main`.\n- Each of the nine controls that I opened fails by assertion: claim reason, claim size, recovery grace, recovery terminal, setup stderr, and four Go descriptor controls. The Go controls retain the compiled test reports.\n- The head changes no file under the kernel, `packages/derive`, `packages/platform` or a Worker.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 736 links, 0 broken.\n- check-commit-messages from 9e313eff: 7 checked, 0 flagged.\n- check-notes from 9e313eff: 0 warnings.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. A record for a different job waits through the grace. The record is complete and its digest matches, so waiting cannot change the result. It costs at most one grace period.\n2. `Delivered.ran` reads `true` after a `claim-too-large` refusal, because the service called `run`. Nothing executed. A view that reads `ran` as \"a container started\" would mislead.\n3. `control-claim-size-1` ran at c6bccda3, against the earlier witness that expected `run-lost`. `control-claim-reason-1` covers the final witness's reason. No control at the final witness removes the size check itself.\n4. Writing:\n   - In `docs/architecture.md`, line 322 is over 79 characters. A line break also falls after \"keeps its job record,\".\n   - Code comments use \"setup/transport\".\n\n## Architecture\n\nRecovery stays inside the runner and the service. The runner classifies a retained record as pending, corrupt or unavailable. The service maps corrupt to `run-lost` and keeps one outcome. Stream handling moves into the immutable helper that the image already carries, so the host's refusal rule does not change. The follow-up adds no kernel operation, Native contract, wire field or Worker change. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\n- Recovery never starts a second execution. It deletes no fence and releases no unauthenticated record.\n- A record for another job never releases its outcome.\n- The helper runs only as UID 65532 and introduces no shell. A failed `exec` cannot pass as a check result, because its fixed stderr line forces the host refusal.\n- The `record-corrupt` log names a private path and copies no record bytes.\n- The signer and credentials stay outside the job, and trusted Git reads keep their fixed environment.\n\nApproved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe follow-up adds one recovery option, one error class each for corruption and claim size, and one helper subcommand. It replaces scattered `refused()` calls with one classification. That is the least the four points needed. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: points 1 to 4 and the claim-too-large witness hold in the code, in the owning tests and in the physical witness. The retained reports and the guard controls verify under ruling 4e199bd8.\n\nThe landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16c953cd0667bc08730970c6a97cf39a2aab33bd","binding":"assigned","head":"7e2d31f3a9da3bae190829fb17e2af12b6e38a58","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f